Firstly you need to know that the AES block function is meant to transform only the data blocks written in the following format:

\begin{code}
Hundreds of zeros preceeded with 8 bits that tell the content length.
\end{code}

Additionally:

\begin{blockquote}
The key should be a block of 16 bytes. However for decryption you can use a 15- or 14- byte key as well. Some decoders even allow you to start with 13. Each insertion of zeros weakens the cypher as vK explains it below:
\end{blockquote}

Thus, when decrypting a cipher with a block taken like this:

\begin{blockquote}
[ tag][block]
\end{blockquote}

you should first use the tag to get the offset in the block that you want to decrypt. When that offset is reached, construct a new block that initially starts with zeroes to fill the place in the block that is currently a tag. Encrypt that new block with the 16 bit block and then put the old tag along with the encrypted block back together.

In the action of using a cbc cypher in this manner, you always write the results of each blocks encryption into the expansion of the next block. So it looks like this:

\begin{blockquote}
[tag][encrypted, 1, plain][encrypted, 2, plain][encrypted, 3, plain]...
\end{blockquote}

Although you could use a ctr cypher instead, changing the way a block is created. Additionally AES doesn't have mathematically proven security against any other knowledge other than the ciphertext.

