With the expected RB pairs thus defined, it remains to compute which proportion of the steps in segment $] {- \pi,- \pi + \theta^* } [ $ are in fact RB pairs.
Denoting $\Theta$ the space of unordered pairs of integers and $\Theta^* $ the set of these pairs such that the integers are different from each other, we get that:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Nbdr}
N_\ell = \sum_{\theta \in]- \pi ,-\pi + \theta^*[} \sum_{\{(i,j) \in \Theta^*, \forall \ k \in [-\ell+1, \ell-1[, \ell \gg 1, \; E_i -E_j =E_k \}} 1
\end{equation}
Equation \eqref{eq:Nbdr} can be easily computed using the properties of Fourier transform.
Noticing that $d_\theta$ is a continuous $2 \pi$-periodic function and that as $E_i$ are centered random variables, equation \eqref{eq:Nbdr} can be approximated by computing the expectation of $d_\theta$ for $\theta \in [- \pi,-\pi + \theta^*[$ and by multiplying it by $\theta^*$.
Typically, when $\alpha$ is small enough $\theta^* = 0.5 \, \pi$ and we get the following relation for example:
\begin{equation}
N = e_0 + \underset{0 < \theta < \pi}{\int} 2 \, \mathbb{E} \left( d_\theta \right) \, d \theta
\end{equation}
